5; ++n) {\n  graf[n] = new picture;\n  for (i = -8; i <= 8; ++i) {\n    draw(graf[n],(i,-8)--(i,8),gray(0.7));\n    draw(graf[n],(-8,i)--(8,i),gray(0.7));\n  }\n  draw(graf[n],(-8,0)--(8,0),Arrows(6));\n  draw(graf[n],(0,-8)--(0,8),Arrows(6));\n\n  label(graf[n],\"$x$\", (8,0), E);\n  label(graf[n],\"$y$\", (0,8), N);\n}\n\ndraw(graf[1],graph(funca,-2,1),red);\ndraw(graf[2],shift((1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[3],shift((1/2,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[4],shift((-1,0))*xscale(1/2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\ndraw(graf[5],shift((-1,0))*xscale(2)*graph(func,-3,3),red);\n\nlabel(graf[1], \"A\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[2], \"B\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[3], \"C\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[4], \"D\", (0,-10));\nlabel(graf[5], \"E\", (0,-10));\n\nadd(graf[1]);\nadd(shift((20,0))*(graf[2]));\nadd(shift((40,0))*(graf[3]));\nadd(shift((10,-20))*(graf[4]));\nadd(shift((30,-20))*(graf[5]));\n[/asy]\n\nEnter the letter of the graph of $y = f(2x + 1).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b84643a014ad5cd0a125540eb0c05a5d1eb76f04d22980990a38d175512898dd"},"seconds":12.82436752319336,"seed":392921997989551190,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the graph of \\( y = f(2x + 1) \\), we need to understand the transformations applied to the original function \\( y = f(x) \\).\n\n1. **Horizontal Compression and Shift**:\n   - The function \\( y = f(2x + 1) \\) involves two transformations:\n     - A horizontal compression by a factor of 2 (because of \\( 2x \\)).\n     - A horizontal shift to the left by 1/2 unit (because of the \\( +1 \\) inside the function).\n\n2. **Horizontal Compression**:\n   - A horizontal compression by a factor of 2 means that every \\( x \\)-coordinate of the points on the graph of \\( y = f(x) \\) is halved. For example, if \\( (a, b) \\) is a point on \\( y = f(x) \\), then \\( (a/2, b) \\) will be a point on \\( y = f(2x) \\).\n\n3. **Horizontal Shift**:\n   - A shift to the left by 1/2 unit means that every \\( x \\)-coordinate of the points on the graph of \\( y = f(2x) \\) is decreased by 1/2. For example, if \\( (a/2, b) \\) is a point on \\( y = f(2x) \\), then \\( (a/2 - 1/2, b) \\) will be a point on \\( y = f(2x + 1) \\).\n\n4. **Analyzing the Options**:\n   - **Graph A**: This graph does not match the transformations. It is not compressed horizontally.\n   - **Graph B**: This graph involves a horizontal compression but no shift. It is not the correct transformation.\n   - **Graph C**: This graph involves a horizontal compression and a horizontal shift to the left by 1/2 unit, which matches our transformations.\n   - **Graph D**: This graph involves a horizontal compression and a horizontal shift to the left by 1 unit, which is incorrect.\n   - **Graph E**: This graph involves a horizontal compression and a horizontal shift to the left by 1 unit, which is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct graph of \\( y = f(2x + 1) \\) is \\(\\boxed{C}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{C}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:421"}
